<h1>Conveyor Cost &amp; Pricing Guide</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Conveyor system costs depend on seven primary factors: conveyor type, length and width, frame material, motor and drive type, controls complexity, environment rating (standard, washdown, cleanroom, hazardous), and installation scope. <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/gravity/">Gravity roller conveyors</a> sit at the low end of the cost spectrum; fully automated packaging lines with PLC controls, servo indexing, and sanitary stainless steel construction sit at the high end. This guide explains each cost factor, provides relative cost comparisons across conveyor types, and lists the questions to ask manufacturers before committing to a quote.</p>
<div class="usa-summary-box">
<div class="usa-summary-box__body">
<div class="usa-summary-box__text">
<h2 id="disclaimer-heading" class="usa-summary-box__heading">Why This Guide Uses Relative Costs, Not Dollar Amounts</h2>
<p>Published conveyor prices become outdated within months due to steel prices, motor availability, labor rates, and regional differences. A dollar figure without context misleads more than it helps. Instead, this guide uses a relative cost scale ($–$$$$) and explains the factors that move a project up or down that scale. Armed with this understanding, you can build a meaningful budget estimate by getting 2–3 quotes from manufacturers for your specific requirements.</p>
</div></div></div>
<h2 id="type-heading">Conveyor Type</h2>
<p>Conveyor type is the single largest cost determinant. A gravity roller section costs a fraction of a servo-driven <a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">indexing conveyor</a> of the same length. The table below ranks common packaging conveyor types from lowest to highest relative cost per linear foot.</p>
