<p>$ = lowest relative cost per linear foot. $$$$ = highest. All comparisons assume standard industrial construction. Sanitary, cleanroom, and hazardous-area ratings shift any type upward.</p>
<h2 id="dims-heading">Length, Width &amp; Height</h2>
<p>Conveyor cost scales roughly linearly with length, double the length, roughly double the cost for the conveyor section (excluding motors, which serve the entire run). Width has a nonlinear effect: wider conveyors require heavier frames, wider belts or more rollers, and larger motors. A 36-inch-wide conveyor costs significantly more than a 12-inch-wide conveyor of the same length and type.</p>
<p>Height (elevation) adds cost through incline/decline sections, support structures, and potentially larger motors to handle the gravitational load. A 10-foot elevation change using an incline belt conveyor requires a longer conveyor run (due to maximum incline angle, typically 15–30 degrees) and a more powerful motor. Spiral conveyors reduce floor space for elevation changes but sit at the top of the cost scale.</p>
<p><strong>Cost optimization tip:</strong> Minimize conveyor length by optimizing packaging line layout. Every foot of unnecessary conveyor adds equipment cost, floor space, and maintenance. A well-planned layout with machines close together and minimal conveyor runs costs less than a sprawling layout with long transport sections. Work with a layout engineer or integrator like Bastian Solutions or Hytrol distribution partners to optimize your floor plan.</p>
<h2 id="materials-heading">Frame &amp; Belt Materials</h2>
<h3>Frame Material</h3>
<p>Frame material is the second-largest cost driver after conveyor type. Three materials cover most packaging applications:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Painted carbon steel ($):</strong> Standard industrial construction. Lowest cost. Adequate for dry environments handling cases, cartons, and totes. Not suitable for washdown or food-contact applications.</li>
<li><strong>Aluminum ($$):</strong> Lightweight, corrosion-resistant, anodized finish. Popular for clean assembly and packaging applications. Extruded aluminum profiles (mk North America, Bosch Rexroth) provide modular construction with T-slot mounting. More expensive than carbon steel but less than stainless.</li>
<li><strong>Stainless steel ($$$):</strong> Required for <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">sanitary food-grade</a> and pharmaceutical applications. 304 stainless steel is standard for most food environments. 316 stainless steel adds cost but resists salt, acid, and aggressive cleaning chemicals. Stainless steel frame construction adds 50–100% over painted carbon steel for the same conveyor type and size.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Belt Material</h3>
<p>Belt material ranges from commodity PVC to specialty silicone:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>PVC ($):</strong> General-purpose belt for dry applications. Not food-grade in most formulations.</li>
<li><strong>Polyurethane ($$):</strong> FDA-approved formulations available for direct food contact. White or blue for visual contamination detection. Standard for <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food conveyors</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Modular plastic ($$–$$$):</strong> Interlocking plastic modules. Easy to repair (replace individual modules). Available in food-grade materials. Higher cost but lower long-term maintenance than flat belts.</li>
<li><strong>Silicone ($$$):</strong> High-temperature resistance for bakery applications (oven discharge, cooling). FDA-approved. Expensive and less durable than polyurethane.</li>
<li><strong>Wire mesh ($$$):</strong> Metal mesh belts for high-temperature or heavy-load applications. Common in baking, cooling, and freezing.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="motors-heading">Motors &amp; Drives</h2>
<p>Motor and drive selection affects both equipment cost and long-term energy cost. The cost hierarchy from lowest to highest:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>No motor, gravity ($):</strong> Gravity conveyors (roller or skatewheel) require no motor. Product moves by gravity or manual push. Lowest equipment and energy cost.</li>
<li><strong>Fixed-speed AC motor ($$):</strong> Standard 3-phase AC motor running at a single speed. Simple and reliable. Cannot adjust speed without mechanical changes (sprocket/pulley swap). Suitable for continuous-run transport sections where speed never changes.</li>
<li><strong>VFD-controlled AC motor ($$$):</strong> A variable frequency drive controls motor speed electronically. Adjustable speed, soft start/stop, and PLC-controlled speed changes. Standard for most <a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">automated conveyor systems</a>. The VFD adds cost beyond the motor itself.</li>
<li><strong>MDR motorized rollers ($$$):</strong> 24VDC brushless motors embedded inside rollers. Each zone has its own motor. Energy efficient (zones run only when product is present). Higher per-zone cost but lower energy and maintenance cost long-term.</li>
<li><strong>Servo drives ($$$$):</strong> Precision motion control for <a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">indexing conveyors</a>. Programmable position, speed, and acceleration profiles. The servo motor plus drive plus controller costs significantly more than a VFD-controlled AC motor. Justified when the application requires precise positioning (±0.5mm or better).</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Washdown motors</strong> (IP69K, stainless steel housing, epoxy-sealed) add a premium over standard enclosed motors. Required in any environment where the conveyor undergoes daily washdown with pressurized water or chemicals.</p>
<h2 id="controls-heading">Controls Complexity</h2>
<p>Controls cost is often the most underestimated line item in a conveyor project. The range from simple to complex is dramatic:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Manual start/stop ($):</strong> Push-button station, motor starter, E-stop. Minimal cost. No sensors, no PLC.</li>
<li><strong>Basic sensor logic ($$):</strong> Photo-eye sensors with relay logic or a micro-PLC. Conveyor starts when product arrives, stops when product leaves. Adequate for standalone conveyor sections not integrated with other machines.</li>
<li><strong>PLC-integrated ($$$):</strong> Allen-Bradley CompactLogix or Siemens S7-1200 PLC with HMI touchscreen. Controls multiple conveyor sections, VFDs, sensors, interlocks, and diagnostics. Includes programming, panel build, wiring, and commissioning. This level represents 15–25% of total system cost.</li>
<li><strong>Full automation ($$$$):</strong> PLC with servo motion, vision integration, <a href="/conveyor-functions/reject-systems/">reject system control</a>, recipe management, data logging, OEE reporting, and network connectivity. Includes extensive programming, system integration testing, and commissioning. This level represents 25–40% of total system cost. See the <a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">automated conveyor systems guide</a> for details.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Critical clarification:</strong> When comparing quotes, verify what "controls" includes. Some vendors quote the conveyor with a motor starter and call it "controls included." Others quote a full PLC package. This scope difference can account for the majority of price variation between quotes for the same conveyor type.</p>
<h2 id="env-heading">Environmental Requirements</h2>
<p>The operating environment multiplies the base cost of any conveyor type:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Standard industrial (baseline):</strong> Dry, room-temperature, non-corrosive environment. Painted carbon steel frames, standard motors, standard bearings. This is the cost baseline, all other environments add premium.</li>
<li><strong>Washdown (+30–60%):</strong> Daily water and chemical washdown. Requires stainless steel frames, IP69K motors, sealed bearings, and food-grade belt material. Open-frame construction for drainage. Dorner's AquaPruf and Shuttleworth's slip-torque <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">sanitary conveyors</a> are built for washdown environments.</li>
<li><strong>Cleanroom (+40–80%):</strong> ISO Class 5–8 cleanroom environments for pharmaceutical and electronics. Low-particulate designs with enclosed motors, smooth surfaces, and specialized lubrication. Minimal exposed fasteners. Higher material and fabrication standards add significant cost.</li>
<li><strong>Hazardous area (+50–100%):</strong> ATEX or Class I/II Division-rated environments (explosive dust or gas). Explosion-proof motors, sealed electrical enclosures, intrinsically safe sensors. Requires third-party certification. The highest-cost environment rating.</li>
<li><strong>Freezer/cold room (+20–40%):</strong> Operating temperatures down to -20°F to -40°F. Cold-rated lubricants, condensation-resistant materials, and motors rated for cold start. Stainless steel is standard to prevent corrosion from condensation cycles.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="install-heading">Installation &amp; Integration</h2>
<p>Installation costs range from 10% to 40% of the equipment cost, depending on site conditions and integration complexity:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mechanical installation:</strong> Unloading, assembling, leveling, anchoring, and connecting conveyor sections. Simple bolt-together conveyors (Hytrol, Dorner) install faster than welded-frame custom conveyors. Budget for rigging equipment if sections exceed forklift capacity.</li>
<li><strong>Electrical installation:</strong> Running power and control cables, connecting motors, wiring sensors, and building or mounting control panels. Electrical scope is proportional to the number of motors, sensors, and the distance between the conveyor and the main electrical panel.</li>
<li><strong>Controls commissioning:</strong> PLC programming, sensor calibration, VFD parameter setup, and integration testing with upstream/downstream machines. Commissioning takes 1–4 weeks for a full packaging line. Requires the equipment vendor's controls engineer on site.</li>
<li><strong>Structural supports:</strong> Floor supports, mezzanine penetrations, overhead supports, and building modifications. If the conveyor runs above existing equipment or through walls, structural engineering adds cost and lead time.</li>
<li><strong>Removal of existing equipment:</strong> If the new conveyor replaces an existing system, demolition and disposal costs apply. Hazardous materials (lead paint, asbestos in old conveyor insulation) require specialized abatement.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Cost optimization tip:</strong> Get installation quotes from both the conveyor manufacturer and independent millwright/electrical contractors. Manufacturers charge higher hourly rates but know their equipment. Independent contractors charge less but may need more time to learn the system. For complex integration, use the manufacturer's team for commissioning and an independent crew for mechanical/electrical installation.</p>
<h2 id="tco-heading">Total Cost of Ownership</h2>
<p>The purchase price represents only 40–60% of a conveyor's lifetime cost. The remaining 40–60% accrues over the equipment's 15–25 year life in energy, maintenance, and downtime:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Energy cost:</strong> <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyors</a> consume 50%+ less energy than powered roller conveyors because zones run only when product is present. VFD-controlled motors save 20–40% vs. fixed-speed motors running continuously. A conveyor running 16 hours/day, 250 days/year accumulates significant energy cost over its life.</li>
<li><strong>Wear part replacement:</strong> Belts, rollers, bearings, chains, and sprockets wear and need periodic replacement. Belt replacement frequency depends on product abrasion, belt material, and operating speed. <a href="/resources/conveyor-belting/">Modular plastic belts</a> cost more upfront but allow section replacement without removing the entire belt.</li>
<li><strong>Labor for maintenance:</strong> Conveyors need preventive maintenance, belt tracking, lubrication, roller inspection, motor cleaning, and sensor alignment. MDR conveyors require less maintenance than belt-driven powered roller conveyors (no belts or chains to adjust). The time your maintenance crew spends on conveyors is time they cannot spend on other equipment.</li>
<li><strong>Downtime cost:</strong> A conveyor failure that stops a packaging line costs the value of lost production per minute of downtime. Reliable conveyors with redundant features (dual drives, quick belt change, modular zone replacement) cost more upfront but prevent costly downtime events.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="questions-heading">Questions to Ask Manufacturers</h2>
<p>Use these questions when requesting and evaluating conveyor quotes. They expose hidden cost differences between vendors and prevent scope surprises:</p>
<h3>Scope &amp; Specification</h3>
<ol>
<li>What is included in "controls", motor starter only, or PLC, HMI, sensors, and VFDs?</li>
<li>What frame material is quoted, carbon steel, aluminum, or stainless? What grade of stainless (304 vs 316)?</li>
<li>What motor type and efficiency rating (IE1, IE2, IE3) is included?</li>
<li>Are sensors, mounting hardware, and wiring included, or are they customer-supplied?</li>
<li>What belt material is quoted, and what is the expected belt life at my operating conditions?</li>
</ol>
<h3>Lead Time &amp; Delivery</h3>
<ol start="6">
<li>What is the lead time from order to ship? Does that include controls panel build?</li>
<li>What is the delivery method, crated freight, flatbed, or vendor installation?</li>
<li>Is pre-assembly available? (Pre-assembled conveyor sections reduce on-site installation time.)</li>
</ol>
<h3>Installation &amp; Commissioning</h3>
<ol start="9">
<li>What installation services are included, mechanical only, or electrical and controls commissioning?</li>
<li>How many days of on-site commissioning are included in the quote?</li>
<li>Who provides operator and maintenance training, and how many hours are included?</li>
</ol>
<h3>Warranty &amp; Support</h3>
<ol start="12">
<li>What is the warranty period (months) and what does it cover, parts only, or parts and labor?</li>
<li>What wear parts are excluded from warranty (belts, rollers, bearings)?</li>
<li>What is the estimated annual cost for wear part replacement at my operating conditions?</li>
<li>What is the response time for warranty service, phone support hours, on-site response commitment?</li>
</ol>
<h3>Future Expansion</h3>
<ol start="16">
<li>Can the system be extended (longer runs, additional sections) in the future?</li>
<li>Does the PLC have spare I/O capacity for future sensors, motors, or integration?</li>
<li>Can the VFDs and motors handle higher speeds or loads if my throughput requirement increases?</li>
</ol>
